// lexdb over TNS: Oracle's assertions (design/lexdb-new-features.md O23 and section 14): CREATE ASSERTION ... CHECK (NOT EXISTS (q) |
// NOT (EXISTS (q)) | EXISTS (q) | ALL (q) SATISFY (c)) with ENABLE / DISABLE, VALIDATE / NOVALIDATE, [NOT] DEFERRABLE, INITIALLY
// IMMEDIATE / DEFERRED; the check after INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE and a PL/SQL block's DML (one table; two tables joined; a
// correlated NOT EXISTS whose DELETE breaks it; an EXISTS; ALL ... SATISFY with NULLs; a self-join; a text key; a table without a
// key; a statement of 200 rows; an UPDATE that fixes one row and breaks another; NOVALIDATE over rows that break it); a deferred
// one failing at COMMIT (ORA-02091) and passing, SET CONSTRAINT[S] ... DEFERRED / IMMEDIATE (ORA-08687); the refusals at CREATE
// (ORA-08689 over 08661, 08695, 08680, 08697, 08677, 08664, 08678, 08673, 08735, 08659, 08660, 08651; ORA-00904, 00942, 00907,
// 00936, 00905, 00906, 00931, 00911, 00922, 00955, 08724, 08701, and rows breaking it); DROP and ALTER ASSERTION (ORA-13650,
// 08725); DDL on the tables it reads (ORA-08702, 08694; DROP TABLE ... CASCADE CONSTRAINTS drops it); USER_ / ALL_ASSERTIONS,
// ASSERTION_DEPENDENCIES, USER_OBJECTS. Checked against Oracle Database Free 23.26.3 (gen_oracle.py --check tns_assert: the answers
// below are Oracle's). Each line's statements run in one transaction, rolled back after (a failing one undone alone, as Oracle's),
